(                           THE GOLD STANDARD.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The most impressive fact in the world of finance is the dominance of) Tj T*
(the gold standard. A year or two ago Roumania passed under its sway,) Tj T*
(to-day it is Austria, next year or soon it will be India, by and by it) Tj T*
(will be Russia, and meanwhile it has lost no ground that it has ever) Tj T*
(held. Three international conferences have been assembled to stay this) Tj T*
(conquering march, while none has been called to promote or assist it.) Tj T*
(Yet the movement has been as little impeded as that of an ocean steamer) Tj T*
(would be by the action of a debating society in its own cabin. Is all) Tj T*
(this due to human perversity, or has it a rational cause founded in the) Tj T*
(needs of mankind?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(                       THE EXPERIENCE OF ENGLAND.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The first nation to adopt the single gold standard by law was England.) Tj T*
(This was really done in 1798, although the date usually assigned to it) Tj T*
(is 1816.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The pound sterling was originally a pound weight of silver, divided) Tj T*
(into twenty parts called shillings, and each of these into twelve parts) Tj T*
(called pennies, or pennyweights. Gold made its first appearance in the) Tj T*
(coinage of England in the reign of Edward III. \(A. D. 1345\). The ratio) Tj T*
(of gold to silver fixed by royal decree in this coinage was about 12½) Tj T*
(to 1.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(From this period to the forty-third year of the reign of Elizabeth) Tj T*
(there were nine debasements of the silver coinage accompanied by) Tj T*
(changes in the gold coinage, but as these were arbitrary acts of) Tj T*
(the reigning sovereigns they possess no scientific interest. In the) Tj T*
(forty-third of Elizabeth \(1601\) the last debasement was made. The) Tj T*
(pound weight of silver was then coined into sixty-two shillings, and) Tj T*
(the pound of gold into thirty-three and one-half sovereigns of seven) Tj T*
(pennyweights and four grains each, the ratio of gold to silver being) Tj T*
(11 to 1. The silver coinage being henceforth unchanged, it becomes) Tj T*
