(possible to trace the commercial variations of the two metals and to) Tj T*
(observe the ineffectual struggles of society and government to keep) Tj T*
(both of them in use as legal-tender money.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Queen Elizabeth died two years later. Before her successor, James I.,) Tj T*
(had been on the throne three years, gold had risen in value as compared) Tj T*
(with silver, and the gold coins were exported to such an extent that it) Tj T*
(was necessary to diminish their weight about 11 per cent. The ratio now) Tj T*
(established was a little more than 12 to 1.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In the ninth year of the same reign the gold coin began to be exported) Tj T*
(again, so that it was necessary to make a new change of ratio. This) Tj T*
(time the ratio was fixed at 13 to 1. But this was too great an advance) Tj T*
(in the rating of gold. An exportation of silver set in which caused) Tj T*
(great inconvenience in the kingdom. Instead of readjusting the ratio) Tj T*
(the King, in the year 1614, issued a proclamation prohibiting the) Tj T*
(exportation of the precious metals. The proclamation had no effect. So) Tj T*
(another one was issued in 1618 reaffirming the first one and forbidding) Tj T*
(the melting of coin for the purpose of making plate, although a certain) Tj T*
(amount might be used for repairing old plate and keeping it up to its) Tj T*
(original standard. As the evil continued a third proclamation was) Tj T*
(issued in 1622 and a fourth in 1624. None of these had any effect) Tj T*
(except to make an historical record of the futility of attempts to) Tj T*
(enforce a legal ratio which is different even in a slight degree from) Tj T*
(the market ratio. It was customary during this period to pay a premium) Tj T*
(of two pence for silver change to the amount of 20 shillings.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Soon after Charles I. began his reign he issued a proclamation on) Tj T*
(the same subject, reciting the previous ones of his father and) Tj T*
(acknowledging that they had been disregarded. In 1636 seven persons) Tj T*
(accused of melting and exporting coin were arrested and fined £8,500) Tj T*
(and imprisoned till the fines were paid, but even this example did not) Tj T*
(put a stop to the practice. Silver was worth two or three pence per) Tj T*
(ounce more than the mint valuation, and this fact dominated society) Tj T*
(from the King on the throne to the beggar on the dunghill. But what) Tj T*
(could not be prevented by royal proclamation and star chamber was) Tj T*
(stopped by an unseen force. The price of gold was slowly rising, so) Tj T*
(that about the beginning of the Commonwealth the ratio that King James) Tj T*
(had established was identical, or nearly so, with the market ratio. The) Tj T*
(exportation of the precious metals ceased until the reign of Charles II.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In 1663 gold had risen in value so that it was necessary to change the) Tj T*
(ratio to 14½ to 1. This was an advance of about 8 per cent. since James) Tj T*
(I.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Each time that a change was made in the gold coinage a new name was) Tj T*
(given to the coin so produced, in order to distinguish it from its) Tj T*
(predecessors. The coin that Charles II. now introduced was called the) Tj T*
(guinea. It was ordered that this coin should pass for twenty shillings,) Tj T*
(but it immediately became current in trade at a higher rate, passing) Tj T*
(for twenty-one to twenty-two shillings. No attempt was made to enforce) Tj T*
(the mint valuation or to prevent melting or exporting. Consequently) Tj T*
