(silver became in practice the only legal-tender money. Nobody would) Tj T*
(offer a guinea to pay a debt of 20 shillings when it was worth 21) Tj T*
(shillings. The guineas passed for what they were worth as bullion. That) Tj T*
(was a time when the clipping of coin was much practiced, but it was no) Tj T*
(advantage to clip a gold coin, since it was taken only at its bullion) Tj T*
(value. The silver coins, however, passed by tale. Consequently they) Tj T*
(alone were subjected to the clipping process. The evil became so great) Tj T*
(that a recoinage of silver was necessary and was undertaken in the) Tj T*
(reign of William III. This was a celebrated event in many ways. Both) Tj T*
(Sir Isaac Newton and John Locke were concerned in it. In the year 1717) Tj T*
(the guinea was made current by royal proclamation at 21 shillings in) Tj T*
(silver, at which figure the ratio was about 15? to 1. This was in the) Tj T*
(third year of the reign of George I.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(It was about this time, says Lord Liverpool, that a marked preference) Tj T*
(was shown by the people for gold money rather than silver, on account) Tj T*
(of its convenience in making large payments. This he ascribes to the) Tj T*
(increase in the commerce of the country. As gold was slightly overrated) Tj T*
(at the ratio of 15? to 1, there was a tendency to export silver. Only) Tj T*
(£584,000 of the latter metal was brought to the mint for a period of) Tj T*
(eighty-three years down to the end of the century, and most of this) Tj T*
(came from Spanish treasure ships captured in war. The only silver coin) Tj T*
(retained in circulation was that which had been much worn. As these) Tj T*
(light-weight pieces varied among themselves, the lightest ones were) Tj T*
(selected to make payments, a condition which became worse and worse) Tj T*
(until Parliament in 1774 passed an act limiting the legal tender of) Tj T*
(silver coins to £25 in tale. For any sum above £25 they could be paid) Tj T*
(by weight only. This act was to continue in force only two years, the) Tj T*
(expectation being that some other remedy for the evil would shortly) Tj T*
(be found. It was re-enacted from time to time till 1798, when another) Tj T*
(clause was added providing that no more silver should be coined at the) Tj T*
(mint, nor should any be delivered that had been coined, but that the) Tj T*
(owners of such silver should be paid for it. In the following year) Tj T*
(\(1799\) a brief act was passed making the act of 1774 perpetual. In) Tj T*
(1816 the character of the British monetary system was formulated by an) Tj T*
(act of Parliament on its present basis, the essential part of this act) Tj T*
(being in the following words:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  ?XI. And whereas at various times heretofore the coins of this realm) Tj T*
(  of gold and silver have been equally a legal tender for payments) Tj T*
(  to any amount, and _great inconvenience has arisen from both those) Tj T*
(  precious metals being concurrently the standard measure of value and) Tj T*
(  equivalent for property_; and it is expedient that the gold coin made) Tj T*
(  according to the indentures of the Mint should henceforth be the) Tj T*
(  sole standard measure of value and legal tender for payment, without) Tj T*
(  any limitation of amount, and that the silver coin should be a legal) Tj T*
(  tender to a limited amount only, for the facility of exchange and) Tj T*
(  commerce;) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  ?Be it therefore enacted, That from and after the passing of this) Tj T*
(  act, the gold coin of this realm shall be and shall be considered and) Tj T*
(  is hereby declared to be the only legal tender for payments, except) Tj T*
