(This is a brief résumé of the experience and legislation of Great) Tj T*
(Britain. It is important as showing that the single gold standard was) Tj T*
(adopted on account of the ?great inconvenience? of the double standard,) Tj T*
(which had been in vogue previously. Of course, this ?inconvenience?) Tj T*
(had attracted the attention of learned men before 1798. John Locke had) Tj T*
(shown that a double standard composed of two things of varying value) Tj T*
(was an impossibility. He favored the single standard of silver, as did) Tj T*
(the learned men who considered the same question in France a century) Tj T*
(later.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(It appears that the gold standard was adopted without any particular) Tj T*
(design on the part of those who brought it about. They found, as a) Tj T*
(matter of fact, that the monetary evils existing in 1774 could be cured) Tj T*
(most readily by limiting the legal tender of silver. So they did it) Tj T*
(for two years, and then for two years more, and so on, till 1798?99,) Tj T*
(when they had become satisfied by the experience of twenty-five years) Tj T*
(that the single gold standard was the right thing to put an end to the) Tj T*
(?inconvenience.? Seventeen years later, the experiment having continued) Tj T*
(to be successful, they passed the law which I have quoted. That law,) Tj T*
(in substance, remains in force to the present time, and we may be sure) Tj T*
(that it would not have lasted so long if it were not a good thing _per) Tj T*
(se_.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(                THE GOLD STANDARD IN THE UNITED STATES.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(We will next consider the experience of the United States. At the) Tj T*
(beginning of our career as a nation we adopted the double standard) Tj T*
(of gold and silver. This was in 1792. Our statesmen followed in this) Tj T*
(matter the example of the older countries of Europe. Alexander) Tj T*
(Hamilton was the Secretary of the Treasury and the ruling spirit. At) Tj T*
(his instance the ratio of 15 was adopted, and there is no room to) Tj T*
(doubt that this was very close to the true market ratio at the time.) Tj T*
(The English ratio of 15? ceased to be operative, as we have seen,) Tj T*
(because it was too high. France was at that time under the régime of) Tj T*
(irredeemable paper. Consequently nothing could be learned from her. The) Tj T*
(discussions and writings of the period show that there was an honest) Tj T*
(and earnest effort to adopt the market ratio as the legal ratio, and) Tj T*
(that the result reached was as nearly true as possible. Nevertheless,) Tj T*
(gold began to grow scarce in our circulation as early as 1810, and had) Tj T*
(wholly disappeared in 1817. One ounce of gold had come to be worth) Tj T*
(as metal something more than fifteen ounces of silver. It was worth) Tj T*
(while for bullion brokers to collect gold coins and export them. The) Tj T*
(testimony is emphatic and is not disputed, that after 1817 and until) Tj T*
(1834 our metallic money consisted of silver exclusively.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
