(                            THE LAW OF 1834.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In 1834 people had become tired of lugging silver around. They had by) Tj T*
(this time found out what was the matter. They determined to have some) Tj T*
(gold in their pockets, but it cannot be affirmed that Congress had) Tj T*
(reached a scientific conclusion in favor of the single gold standard.) Tj T*
(What is certain is that Congress adopted the ratio of 16 to 1 in 1834) Tj T*
(by very large majorities in spite of proofs urgently presented that) Tj T*
(this ratio would drive silver out of circulation altogether, as it did.) Tj T*
(This bill was called the ?Gold Bill? in the discussions of the time.) Tj T*
(As reported by the special committee, it provided for a ratio of 15.60) Tj T*
(to 1, but when it came up for discussion, Mr. Campbell P. White, the) Tj T*
(Chairman of the Committee, who was himself in favor of the single gold) Tj T*
(standard, moved to amend by making the ratio 16 to 1, and his amendment) Tj T*
(was adopted without a division. On the main question the debate was) Tj T*
(long and animated. An amendment to the amendment was offered, making) Tj T*
(the ratio 15.625 to 1, and it was supported on the ground that this was) Tj T*
(the true market ratio, and that it would enable the country to keep) Tj T*
(both silver and gold in concurrent circulation. This amendment was) Tj T*
(voted down--yeas 52, nays 127. The bill was then passed in the House by) Tj T*
(145 to 36, and in the Senate by 35 to 7.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(There was a variety of motives leading to the passage of the Gold Bill,) Tj T*
(but among these the desire of having gold in place of silver was the) Tj T*
(most influential. Thomas H. Benton, one of the strongest advocates of) Tj T*
(the measure, declared that the object of his endeavors was:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  ?To enable the friends of gold to go to work at the right place to) Tj T*
(  effect the recovery of that precious metal which their fathers once) Tj T*
(  possessed, which the subjects of European kings now possess, which) Tj T*
(  the citizens of the young republics to the south all possess, which) Tj T*
(  even the free negroes of San Domingo possess, but which the yeomanry) Tj T*
(  of this America have been deprived for more than twenty years, and) Tj T*
(  will be deprived forever unless they discover the cause of the evil) Tj T*
(  and apply the remedy to its root.--[Speech of Senator Benton of) Tj T*
(  Missouri, quoted by Louis R. Ehrich in his ?Question of Silver.?]?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The effect that was predicted was abundantly realized. Silver did go) Tj T*
(out of circulation. The minor coins, being of proportional weight and) Tj T*
(fineness with the dollar, were melted and exported, and their place in) Tj T*
(the circulation was taken by light-weight foreign coins, principally) Tj T*
(Spanish and Mexican six-pences, shillings, quarters, and halves.) Tj T*
(Those coins, when of full weight, were almost identical with our own) Tj T*
(fractional coins. If our own would not circulate the foreign ones) Tj T*
(of course would not. But if there was a certain proportion of these) Tj T*
(coins, whether foreign or domestic, that had been worn down by long use) Tj T*
(so that they really represented the market ratio or something less,) Tj T*
(such coins would circulate concurrently with gold. To illustrate: two) Tj T*
(halves, four quarters, or ten dimes, if new and of full weight, were) Tj T*
(worth about one cent and a half more than a gold dollar. Consequently) Tj T*
(they would be collected by brokers, melted and exported. But two) Tj T*
(halves, four quarters, or ten dimes, that had lost one and a half) Tj T*
