(cents? worth of silver by abrasion, would circulate, because there) Tj T*
(would be no motive to melt or export them. There would be no profit) Tj T*
(in it. When I was a boy the silver money of this country consisted) Tj T*
(exclusively of foreign coins, mostly Spanish and Mexican, but with a) Tj T*
(considerable sprinkling of English, French, German and Scandinavian) Tj T*
(pieces. Every merchant kept a coin chart manual for handy reference to) Tj T*
(determine the value of these pieces as they were offered in trade. I) Tj T*
(have also seen Spanish quarters cut in half, each piece circulating as) Tj T*
(a shilling. There was nothing remarkable about this, since all these) Tj T*
(foreign coins were circulating at their bullion value. The two halves) Tj T*
(of a Spanish quarter were therefore worth as much as they would have) Tj T*
(been if joined in a single piece.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(It became apparent to everybody that if full-weight silver coins would) Tj T*
(not circulate on the ratio of 16 to 1, while those of light weight) Tj T*
(would circulate, then it would be safe to _make_ minor coins \(halves,) Tj T*
(quarters, etc.\), designedly of light weight on Government account, of) Tj T*
(limited legal tender. There would be no profit in exporting such coins,) Tj T*
(because they would not sell as bullion for as much as it would cost to) Tj T*
(collect them. In 1853 an act of this kind was passed.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(From 1837 onward the country had gold money and the gold basis. Silver) Tj T*
(dollars were hardly ever seen. There was not an hour in the whole) Tj T*
(period of forty years to 1873 when the silver dollar was not worth more) Tj T*
(than the gold dollar. With the exception of a very few years it was) Tj T*
(worth fully three cents more. Did any of you ever see a silver dollar) Tj T*
(in circulation prior to 1878? I never did.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(                            THE LAW OF 1873.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Under these circumstances, the gold standard existing _de facto_, and) Tj T*
(there being no silver except light-weight subsidiary coins, our mint) Tj T*
(authorities, the only people who took any interest in the subject,) Tj T*
(began even before the war to recommend that the single gold standard) Tj T*
(should be adopted in law as it had been adopted in fact. Ex-Gov.) Tj T*
(Pollock, Director of the Mint, in his report for 1861 called attention) Tj T*
(to the incongruity of a silver dollar that was worth 3.10 cents more) Tj T*
(than the gold dollar and 8 cents more than two half dollars. He) Tj T*
(recommended that it should either be dropped from the list of coins) Tj T*
(or reduced in weight so as to correspond with the subsidiary coins.) Tj T*
(He considered that gold was _de facto_ the standard of value, and he) Tj T*
(recommended that the law should conform to the fact. But the nation) Tj T*
(had more exciting topics to discuss in 1861 than those relating to) Tj T*
(coinage. In 1866, after the war, Mr. John J. Knox, who then had) Tj T*
(charge of the Mint and coinage matters in the Treasury Department,) Tj T*
(recommended a revision of all the laws relating to the Mint. Secretary) Tj T*
(Boutwell approved of the suggestion. Mr. Knox and Dr. Linderman were) Tj T*
(appointed in 1869 a committee to make such revision. They presented) Tj T*
(their report with a draft of a bill in 1870. The report recommended the) Tj T*
(discontinuance of the silver dollar, this coin being obsolete.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
