(The bill and report were transmitted to the Finance Committee of the) Tj T*
(Senate on the 25th of April, 1870. The bill passed the Senate on the) Tj T*
(10th of January, 1871. It made the gold dollar the unit of value and) Tj T*
(it dropped the silver dollar from the list of coins. The bill failed) Tj T*
(in the House for want of time. The Forty-first Congress having expired) Tj T*
(without final action it came up again in the Forty-second. It passed) Tj T*
(the House May 27, 1872, by yeas 110, nays 13. It passed the Senate) Tj T*
(January 17, 1873, without a dissenting vote. The metal in the silver) Tj T*
(dollar at that time was worth two cents more than the gold dollar. No) Tj T*
(objection to the bill was heard until the price of silver had fallen so) Tj T*
(that the silver dollar, if there had been any, would have been worth) Tj T*
(less than the gold dollar. Then it became fashionable to say that) Tj T*
(the bill was passed surreptitiously. The truth is, that the bill was) Tj T*
